window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 — Communication Skills","h":"","i":"ccs-101-communication-skills","x":"CCS 101 — Communication Skills University of Embu · Humanities This pack organizes the available course outline and lecture materials into a topic-by-topic study path. Search covers the text inside each note. Use the progress tracker to mark a topic complete only after you can explain it without looking."},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 — Communication Skills","h":"Learning goals","i":"learning-goals","x":"Learning goals Work through each topic in order, or search for a specific term or example. Use the interactive recall checklist at the end of each note. Record confidence and revision dates in the progress tracker. Consult the source material when a note marks a gap or an equation needs checking."},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 — Communication Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes 01 · Fundamentals of Communication 02 · Communication and Collaboration 03 · Study Skills 04 · Writing Skills"},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 — Communication Skills","h":"How to study","i":"how-to-study","x":"How to study Read one topic and mark its checklist as you complete each activity. Close the notes and write the key ideas from memory. Use search or “Find in note” to resolve specific gaps. Update your confidence and next review date in the progress tracker."},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"","i":"your-study-plan","x":"Your study plan"},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"Weekly study cycle","i":"weekly-study-cycle","x":"Weekly study cycle Preview the course outline and choose this week's topics. Study one or two notes per session. Do a closed-book recall after each note. Revisit weak topics within 48 hours. Complete a mixed-topic review at the end of the week."},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"Exam-week cycle","i":"exam-week-cycle","x":"Exam-week cycle Review the progress tracker and prioritize low-confidence topics. Rework examples without looking at their solutions. Search the notes for definitions and distinctions you cannot recall. Complete the question bank and record errors in the tracker. Protect time for sleep and a final light review."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 · Exam-day review","h":"","i":"ccs-101-exam-day-review","x":"CCS 101 · Exam-day review For most answers use: define → explain the process or principle → apply it to an example → evaluate or conclude ."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 · Exam-day review","h":"Communication and collaboration","i":"communication-and-collaboration","x":"Communication and collaboration Process: source/participant → encode → message → channel → receiver → decode → feedback , shaped by context and possible noise. Do not confuse message (content) with channel (medium). 7 Cs: clarity, completeness, conciseness, consistency, correctness, consideration, courtesy. Listening: attend, interpret, check meaning (paraphrase / clarify), respond. Group work: shared goal, roles, deadlines, communication, integration, review. Oral presentation: audience and purpose → organized points/evidence → rehearse → deliver clearly → respond to questions. Reading: skimming for overview; scanning for a detail; close reading to evaluate an argument."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 · Exam-day review","h":"Study and information literacy","i":"study-and-information-literacy","x":"Study and information literacy SQ3R: Survey, Question, Read, Recite/Recall, Review. Active revision: retrieve from memory, check, correct, schedule delayed practice. Evaluate sources: author, evidence, relevance, currency, purpose/perspective; record citation details as you take notes. Paraphrase genuinely and cite borrowed ideas; changing a few words is not sufficient."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 · Exam-day review","h":"Writing","i":"writing","x":"Writing Process: plan → draft → revise meaning/structure → edit sentences → proofread details. Paragraph: topic sentence + support/evidence/example + explanation/link. Essay: focused introduction/thesis → ordered body points → conclusion that answers the task. Quote sparingly and accurately; acknowledge sources in the required style."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 · Exam-day review","h":"Common mark-losing errors","i":"common-mark-losing-errors","x":"Common mark-losing errors Listing terms without explaining or applying them. Assuming communication is one-way or that the audience interprets every message identically. Treating rereading as proof of recall. Making claims without evidence or citation. Editing spelling while leaving an unclear argument unchanged. Use the question bank , check the answer guide , and record weak points in the progress tracker ."},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 · Progress tracker and weak-point log","h":"","i":"ccs-101-progress-tracker-and-weak-point-log","x":"CCS 101 · Progress tracker and weak-point log Attempt linked questions closed-book. Score 0–3 and confidence 1–5 per topic. Note the exact gap, choose a next action, and schedule a retry. Topic Note Questions Score /3 Confidence /5 Weak point / action / review date 01 · Fundamentals Note C01-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 02 · Collaboration and listening Note C02-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 03 · Study skills Note C03-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 04 · Writing skills Note C04-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 Review rule: 3/3 with confidence 4–5 → delayed mixed recall; 2/3 or confidence 2–3 → repair the specific gap and retry missed questions; 0–1/3 or confidence 1 → revisit the note and redo all three questions. Date Topic / question Exact weak point Next action Retry date / result"},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"","i":"01-fundamentals-of-communication","x":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication Study focus: what communication is, how meaning moves, why context matters, and how to make messages effective. Based on the CCS 101 course outline and Topic 1 lecture notes."},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"1. Communication is a process","i":"1-communication-is-a-process","x":"1. Communication is a process Communication is the purposeful exchange of ideas, information, knowledge, attitudes, or feelings through shared symbols, signs, words, and behaviour. It is more than sending information: participants interpret meaning and respond."},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"A practical process model","i":"a-practical-process-model","x":"A practical process model Participant/source → encode → message → channel → participant/receiver → decode → feedback Participants may send, receive, or alternate roles. Their knowledge, interests, and emotional state affect the exchange. Encoding puts an idea into words, signs, images, or behaviour; decoding interprets those signs. The message is the content; the channel is how it travels (for example, speech, writing, or a graphic). Feedback is the receiver’s verbal, written, or non-verbal response. It helps show whether meaning was understood. Context includes the situation, time and place, physical environment, and participants’ backgrounds and relationships. Example: A student emails a lecturer to ask for a deadline clarification. The student encodes a specific question in writing; the email is the channel; the lecturer decodes it and replies. If the answer is unclear, the student asks a focused follow-up."},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"2. Communication principles and types","i":"2-communication-principles-and-types","x":"2. Communication principles and types Inescapable: communication occurs at intrapersonal and interpersonal levels; even choosing not to speak can convey a response. Irreversible: once a message is received, it cannot be fully taken back. Dynamic: meaning and interaction can change as participants respond. Contextual: the same words or behaviour can be interpreted differently in different situations. Complicated: communicators make choices about purpose, wording, timing, audience, and channel. Classify communication in two useful ways: Classification Types Example By participants Intrapersonal; interpersonal (dyadic or small group); public; mass Thinking through a decision; a conversation; a speech; a broadcast By channel/form Oral/verbal; written; graphic/visual; non-verbal Discussion; email; chart; gesture or facial expression Forms can work together. A presentation may combine spoken explanation, slides, and gestures."},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"3. Make messages effective: the 7 Cs","i":"3-make-messages-effective-the-7-cs","x":"3. Make messages effective: the 7 Cs Use the course’s 7 Cs as a message check: Clarity: make the intended meaning easy to understand. Completeness: include the information the audience needs to act. Conciseness: remove repetition and unnecessary wording. Consistency: keep details and claims aligned. Correctness: check facts, names, grammar, and tone. Consideration: adapt to the audience’s needs and perspective. Courtesy: communicate respectfully. Revision example: “Send me that thing soon” is vague. “Please email me the draft report by 3 p.m. Thursday so I can review it” is clearer and more complete."},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"4. Barriers and practical responses","i":"4-barriers-and-practical-responses","x":"4. Barriers and practical responses Barrier How it interferes Useful response Noise or interruptions Important parts are missed Choose a quieter place; pause and restate key information Lack of knowledge or unclear wording Terms are misunderstood Define terms; ask a clarifying question Strong emotion or low interest Attention and interpretation are affected Pause, listen without jumping to conclusions, and return to the purpose Stereotyping or assumed agreement The message is filtered through an unfair assumption Check the evidence and ask the person what they mean Difficulty expressing an idea The receiver cannot follow the point Plan the main point and use a suitable example or channel"},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"5. Put it into practice","i":"5-put-it-into-practice","x":"5. Put it into practice"},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Before sending or speaking","i":"before-sending-or-speaking","x":"Before sending or speaking What is my purpose, and what response do I need? Who is the audience, and what do they already know? Is this channel appropriate for the message? Is the message clear, complete, concise, consistent, correct, considerate, and courteous? How will I check understanding?"},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Active practice","i":"active-practice","x":"Active practice Choose a real campus announcement. Rewrite it for two audiences (for example, first-year students and a course instructor). Name the purpose, context, channel, and likely feedback for each version. Explain which of the 7 Cs you changed and why."},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Common pitfalls","i":"common-pitfalls","x":"Common pitfalls Treating communication as one-way delivery instead of exchange and feedback. Assuming a message has the same meaning for every audience. Confusing the channel (email) with the message (the request in the email). Using vague wording, stereotypes, or an unsuitable channel without checking the result. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Collaboration, Listening, Oral and Reading Skills","h":"","i":"02-collaboration-listening-oral-and-reading-skills","x":"02 · Collaboration, Listening, Oral and Reading Skills Study focus: work productively with others, listen for meaning, take useful notes, and communicate ideas aloud and in writing. Based on Lesson 2 and the CCS 101 course outline."},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Collaboration, Listening, Oral and Reading Skills","h":"1. Collaboration: make shared work visible","i":"1-collaboration-make-shared-work-visible","x":"1. Collaboration: make shared work visible Collaboration is two or more people working together toward a shared task or goal. It can support teamwork, communication, innovation, and effective delivery when members contribute and coordinate."},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Collaboration, Listening, Oral and Reading Skills","h":"A simple group-work cycle","i":"a-simple-group-work-cycle","x":"A simple group-work cycle Agree on the outcome: turn the assignment into a clear shared goal. Plan the work: break it into tasks, owners, and realistic deadlines. Share information: listen, contribute evidence, and keep notes of decisions. Check progress: ask what is complete, what is blocked, and who needs help. Combine and review: check that the parts fit together and represent the group’s agreed work. Reflect: identify what helped the group and what to change next time. Example: For a short research presentation, members can agree on the question, assign source-finding and slide tasks, set a merge deadline, and rehearse together. A named owner and due date make each task trackable."},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Collaboration, Listening, Oral and Reading Skills","h":"Collaboration checklist","i":"collaboration-checklist","x":"Collaboration checklist Goal, roles, and deadlines are understood by everyone. Each member has a meaningful contribution. Decisions and sources are recorded and shared. Disagreement is about ideas and evidence, not personal attacks. The final work is reviewed as one coherent product."},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Collaboration, Listening, Oral and Reading Skills","h":"2. Listening is active meaning-making","i":"2-listening-is-active-meaning-making","x":"2. Listening is active meaning-making Hearing is receiving sound. Listening is the deliberate process of attending to, interpreting, evaluating, and responding to a message. Listening supports learning, memory, relationships, and fewer misunderstandings. Listening can serve different purposes: Active: focus on the speaker and show attention through relevant feedback. Reflective: restate or paraphrase the meaning to check understanding. Critical: examine reasons and evidence before accepting a claim. Empathic: attend to the speaker’s feelings and perspective. Enjoyment: listen for appreciation or pleasure. Passive: receive sound with little active engagement; important details can be missed. These are useful modes, not a single ranking. Choose the mode that fits the purpose."},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Collaboration, Listening, Oral and Reading Skills","h":"Listen in four moves","i":"listen-in-four-moves","x":"Listen in four moves Prepare: reduce distractions and notice your purpose. Attend: let the speaker finish; observe words and relevant non-verbal cues. Check meaning: ask an open or clarifying question; paraphrase important points. Respond: give a relevant answer or record key ideas. Example: “So the proposal is due Friday, and you want feedback on the outline by Wednesday—is that right?” This paraphrase checks two details before action. Common barriers include environmental noise, stress or anger, tiredness or hearing difficulty, and unfamiliar terms or jargon. Reduce distractions where possible, manage your state, ask for a term to be explained, and request repetition or another channel when needed."},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Collaboration, Listening, Oral and Reading Skills","h":"3. Take notes that help you retrieve ideas","i":"3-take-notes-that-help-you-retrieve-ideas","x":"3. Take notes that help you retrieve ideas Capture main ideas, supporting details, examples, questions, and source information —not every sentence. Record the date, topic, and presenter/source when relevant. Use abbreviations and symbols consistently, then expand anything unclear soon after the session. Format Best use How it works Cornell Lecture or reading with later review Main notes on one side, cue questions on the other, brief summary below Outline Hierarchical material Indent supporting points under main ideas Charting Comparing repeated categories Use columns for categories such as claim, evidence, and example Mind map Showing relationships Put the central topic in the middle and connect related branches After a lecture, compare notes with a classmate to identify gaps, but verify details against the source rather than copying errors."},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Collaboration, Listening, Oral and Reading Skills","h":"4. Oral skills: plan, deliver, respond","i":"4-oral-skills-plan-deliver-respond","x":"4. Oral skills: plan, deliver, respond An oral presentation is easier to follow when one clear purpose guides the structure. Plan: identify audience, purpose, time limit, and key message. Organize: introduce the topic and main point; develop a few logically ordered points with examples; conclude by summarizing or stating the next step. Prepare: rehearse aloud, check timing, and make any visual aid readable and relevant. Deliver: use an audible pace and clear wording; look up from notes; use gestures naturally. Respond: listen to questions fully, clarify if needed, and answer directly. If unsure, say so and identify how you would verify the point. Example outline: Purpose: explain a library search strategy. Opening: present the research question. Body: show keywords, filters, and source checks. Close: summarize the search and ask for questions."},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Collaboration, Listening, Oral and Reading Skills","h":"5. Reading for purpose","i":"5-reading-for-purpose","x":"5. Reading for purpose Preview headings, introductions, conclusions, diagrams, and layout to see how a text is organized. Set a question that tells you what information to locate. Read actively: identify the main claim, supporting reasons, definitions, and evidence. Check meaning: pause to explain a paragraph in your own words; mark unfamiliar terms to verify. Review: summarize the argument and note questions or useful quotations with page details. Skimming gives an overview; scanning searches for a particular item. Neither replaces close reading when you must evaluate an argument or use evidence."},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Collaboration, Listening, Oral and Reading Skills","h":"Active practice","i":"active-practice","x":"Active practice Listen to a short course explanation and take outline notes. Close the source and give a one-minute summary from memory. Then check your notes: mark one main idea, two supporting details, one question, and any missed or misheard point."},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Collaboration, Listening, Oral and Reading Skills","h":"Common pitfalls","i":"common-pitfalls","x":"Common pitfalls Interrupting or planning your reply while the other person is still speaking. Treating group participation as task division only, with no coordination or shared review. Writing a transcript instead of notes that distinguish main ideas from examples. Treating skimming as enough to evaluate evidence or prepare an argument. Reading slides aloud rather than explaining their meaning to the audience. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills and Information Literacy","h":"","i":"03-study-skills-and-information-literacy","x":"03 · Study Skills and Information Literacy Study focus: plan learning, engage actively, retain and retrieve information, and use academic sources responsibly. Based on Topic 5 study-skills notes and the CCS 101 course outline."},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills and Information Literacy","h":"1. Study actively","i":"1-study-actively","x":"1. Study actively Studying is learning through reading, reflection, and practice so information can be understood, retained, recalled, and applied. Study skills are the methods and habits that support those steps. Passive learning: mainly listen, reread, or memorize without testing understanding. Active learning: ask questions, connect ideas, evaluate information, explain concepts, and practise retrieval."},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills and Information Literacy","h":"A repeatable study session","i":"a-repeatable-study-session","x":"A repeatable study session Choose one specific outcome (for example, explain the communication process without notes). Preview the material and turn headings into questions. Study a manageable section using focused reading and useful notes. Close the material; retrieve the answer aloud or in writing. Check and correct gaps; use a practice question or example. Schedule a later review and adjust your plan based on what remains difficult."},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills and Information Literacy","h":"2. Read to remember: SQ3R and PQ4R","i":"2-read-to-remember-sq3r-and-pq4r","x":"2. Read to remember: SQ3R and PQ4R SQ3R turns reading into a question-and-recall process: Survey: preview titles, headings, diagrams, and summaries. Question: make questions from the headings. Read: look for answers and supporting details. Recite/Recall: explain the main points without looking. Review: check the source, correct gaps, and revisit later. PQ4R adds reflection: Preview the material. Question what you need to learn. Read to find answers. Reflect on meaning and connections. Recite key ideas from memory. Review the material and correct omissions. Example: For a section on listening barriers, preview the headings, ask “What kinds of barriers are there?”, read for categories and examples, then list each category and one response from memory."},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills and Information Literacy","h":"3. Plan time and protect concentration","i":"3-plan-time-and-protect-concentration","x":"3. Plan time and protect concentration A study schedule is a time-management plan that assigns study sessions to learning goals. Planning is ongoing: track actual use of time, make a daily or weekly schedule, set mini-deadlines, review completed work, and revise the plan when tasks or deadlines change."},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills and Information Literacy","h":"Build a workable schedule","i":"build-a-workable-schedule","x":"Build a workable schedule List deadlines and the tasks needed to meet them. Estimate time; break large work into smaller sessions. Put demanding or priority work into available focused periods. Include breaks and other commitments. At day’s end, compare planned with completed work and reset priorities. The course notes describe the Pomodoro technique as 25 minutes of focused work followed by a 5-minute break. Treat this as one possible structure; adjust the interval to the task and your needs. For concentration, identify the actual distraction (noise, phone, unclear goal, or fatigue) and choose a matching response: change the environment, set a specific mini-goal, remove a distraction, or take an appropriate break."},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills and Information Literacy","h":"4. Memory: encode, store, retrieve","i":"4-memory-encode-store-retrieve","x":"4. Memory: encode, store, retrieve The study notes describe three stages: Sensory memory briefly registers incoming stimuli; attention helps select information for further processing. Short-term memory (STM) holds a limited amount for a short time. Long-term memory (LTM) stores learned information for later retrieval. The practical study cycle is encode → store/organize → retrieve . Make meaning and connections, organize information, then practise recalling it rather than only rereading. Memory aids from the course notes include: Chunking: group related details into meaningful units. Acronyms or mnemonic sentences: use initials as retrieval cues (for example, the familiar “ROYGBIV” for the rainbow colour sequence). Visual association: connect a term or name to a memorable image. Practice retrieval: explain, write, or answer questions from memory, then check accuracy."},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills and Information Literacy","h":"5. Information literacy and ethical use","i":"5-information-literacy-and-ethical-use","x":"5. Information literacy and ethical use Information literacy means recognizing an information need, finding relevant information, evaluating it, and using it responsibly. It supports course research, assignments, presentations, and lifelong learning."},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills and Information Literacy","h":"Research workflow","i":"research-workflow","x":"Research workflow Define the need: turn the assignment into a focused research question; note its scope and required source types. Search deliberately: use suitable library catalogues, databases, books, or other course-relevant resources. Try key terms and related terms; record where you searched. Evaluate before relying on a source: check who created it, its evidence and references, relevance to your question, currency when dates matter, and its purpose or perspective. Take traceable notes: distinguish your own ideas from source wording; record author, title, date, and page or location while reading. Use and acknowledge sources: paraphrase genuinely in your own structure and language; quote exact wording only when useful and mark it as a quotation; cite ideas and words that are not your own using the required course style. Present findings: organize evidence around the research question and explain how it supports your conclusions. Plagiarism and cheating are unacceptable under the course outline. A citation does not make an inaccurate claim reliable, and changing a few words does not turn copied wording into a genuine paraphrase."},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills and Information Literacy","h":"Active practice","i":"active-practice","x":"Active practice Use one course question to make a one-week study plan with three short sessions. In the first session, create SQ3R questions; in the second, answer them from memory; in the third, check sources and explain one remaining weak point. Separately, find one library or academic source and record its author, purpose, evidence, relevance, and citation details."},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills and Information Literacy","h":"Common pitfalls","i":"common-pitfalls","x":"Common pitfalls Scheduling study time without naming what you will learn or practise. Rereading as the only revision method, with no recall or application. Treating a search result’s ranking as proof of reliability. Copying notes without recording their source, or paraphrasing too closely. Making a plan once and not adjusting it when deadlines or workload change. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"","i":"04-writing-skills","x":"04 · Writing Skills"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Writing is a process","i":"writing-is-a-process","x":"Writing is a process Effective academic writing communicates a focused purpose to a particular reader. It is usually produced through planning → drafting → revising → editing → proofreading . Revising improves meaning and organization; editing improves sentence clarity and correctness; proofreading catches final surface errors."},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Build a clear paragraph","i":"build-a-clear-paragraph","x":"Build a clear paragraph A useful paragraph normally has: Topic sentence: states the paragraph’s main point. Support: explains the point with evidence, reasoning, or an example. Link: connects the evidence to the claim or transitions to the next point. Example skeleton: Claim: Active recall supports revision. Evidence/example: A student answers a question from memory before checking notes. Explanation: This reveals what can be retrieved and what still needs review."},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Essay structure","i":"essay-structure","x":"Essay structure Introduction: establish context, define key terms if needed, and state the focus or thesis. Body: organize paragraphs around distinct points; support claims with relevant evidence and explain its significance. Conclusion: answer the task directly, synthesize the main reasoning, and avoid introducing unsupported new claims. Use transitions to show relationships—contrast, cause, sequence, or addition—not merely to decorate the page."},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Academic integrity and sources","i":"academic-integrity-and-sources","x":"Academic integrity and sources Keep traceable source details while researching. Distinguish quotation, paraphrase, and your own analysis. Quote exact words accurately and sparingly; paraphrase by genuinely restating the idea in your own structure and language; cite borrowed ideas and wording using the required style. A citation does not excuse a misleading or unsupported claim."},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Editing checklist","i":"editing-checklist","x":"Editing checklist Does each paragraph serve the question and one main point? Are claims supported and sources acknowledged? Are sentences direct, complete, and logically connected? Are grammar, spelling, names, figures, and references checked? Does the final format follow the assignment instructions?"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Distinguish revising from proofreading. Draft a topic sentence and two supporting sentences for one course claim. Explain how to paraphrase without patchwriting. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"","i":"ccs-101-topic-linked-question-bank","x":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank Answer from memory, using examples where possible. Then use the answer guide and log a concrete action in the tracker ."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"01 · Fundamentals of communication","i":"01-fundamentals-of-communication","x":"01 · Fundamentals of communication"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"C01-Q1","i":"c01-q1","x":"C01-Q1 Draw or describe the communication process and explain the role of feedback and context."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"C01-Q2","i":"c01-q2","x":"C01-Q2 Distinguish message from channel, using an email example."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"C01-Q3","i":"c01-q3","x":"C01-Q3 Rewrite “Send me that thing soon” as a clear, complete, courteous request. Explain two changes."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"02 · Collaboration, listening, oral and reading skills","i":"02-collaboration-listening-oral-and-reading-skills","x":"02 · Collaboration, listening, oral and reading skills"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"C02-Q1","i":"c02-q1","x":"C02-Q1 Distinguish hearing from listening and outline four moves for active listening."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"C02-Q2","i":"c02-q2","x":"C02-Q2 A group has duplicated work and missed a deadline. Propose a practical collaboration plan."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"C02-Q3","i":"c02-q3","x":"C02-Q3 Distinguish skimming, scanning, and close reading; state when each is useful."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"03 · Study skills and information literacy","i":"03-study-skills-and-information-literacy","x":"03 · Study skills and information literacy"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"C03-Q1","i":"c03-q1","x":"C03-Q1 Reproduce SQ3R and explain how recall differs from rereading."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"C03-Q2","i":"c03-q2","x":"C03-Q2 Give a realistic three-session revision plan that includes retrieval practice and delayed review."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"C03-Q3","i":"c03-q3","x":"C03-Q3 Evaluate a source using author, evidence, relevance, currency, and purpose; explain how you would cite it."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"04 · Writing skills","i":"04-writing-skills","x":"04 · Writing skills"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"C04-Q1","i":"c04-q1","x":"C04-Q1 Distinguish revising, editing, and proofreading."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"C04-Q2","i":"c04-q2","x":"C04-Q2 Write a paragraph plan with a topic sentence, support, and explanation for a claim about active study."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"C04-Q3","i":"c04-q3","x":"C04-Q3 Explain how to paraphrase and cite an idea without patchwriting."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Answer guide","h":"","i":"ccs-101-answer-guide","x":"CCS 101 · Answer guide Use these points to check understanding. Strong answers use accurate terms and a relevant example."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Answer guide","h":"01 · Fundamentals","i":"01-fundamentals","x":"01 · Fundamentals C01-Q1: Participant/source encodes a message through a channel; receiver decodes and gives feedback. Context and noise affect interpretation; participants can exchange roles. C01-Q2: Message is the content/request; channel is email. Example: the message asks for a deadline clarification; email carries it. C01-Q3: Example: “Please email me the draft report by 3 p.m. Thursday so I can review it.” It identifies the item, action, deadline, purpose, and courteous tone."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Answer guide","h":"02 · Collaboration, listening, oral and reading","i":"02-collaboration-listening-oral-and-reading","x":"02 · Collaboration, listening, oral and reading C02-Q1: Hearing receives sound; listening attends to, interprets, evaluates, and responds. Prepare, attend, check meaning, respond. C02-Q2: Agree on one outcome, assign owners and due dates, record decisions, check progress, integrate work, and review together. C02-Q3: Skim for overview; scan for a particular detail; close-read to understand and evaluate reasoning/evidence."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Answer guide","h":"03 · Study and information literacy","i":"03-study-and-information-literacy","x":"03 · Study and information literacy C03-Q1: Survey, Question, Read, Recite/Recall, Review. Retrieval requires producing information without looking; rereading mainly presents it again. C03-Q2: A valid plan has a specific outcome, an initial learning/retrieval session, a later closed-book retry, and feedback/correction. C03-Q3: Evaluate creator expertise, supporting evidence, relevance to the task, date when currency matters, and purpose/perspective; record details and cite using the required style."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 101 · Answer guide","h":"04 · Writing","i":"04-writing","x":"04 · Writing C04-Q1: Revising improves argument/structure; editing improves wording and sentence correctness; proofreading catches final typographical and formatting errors. C04-Q2: A focused claim followed by relevant support/evidence/example and explanation linking it back to the claim. C04-Q3: Understand the source, restate its idea in a genuinely new sentence structure and wording, preserve meaning, and cite the source. Quote exact wording only when clearly marked and cited."}];